    "date_str": "2024-09-04",
    "description": "Hansard of September 04, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1726136307029282.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-09-04-2024-09-04-0235",
    "chunk_index": 235,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "b9f581b8730f629a2f937cbd18d25afe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "Next, we must embrace sustainable land use and agriculture. We can explore crop diversification by planting crops less attractive to elephants such as chillies and citrus in the border areas of farmland. By shifting to community-based agriculture practices, we can adopt elephant-friendly methods like using beehive fences. This method has proven effective everywhere, as elephants tend to avoid bees.\n\nEarly warning systems are another innovation we must embrace. By using mobile technology to track elephant movements, we can alert villagers when elephants are approaching, allowing them time to take precautionary measures. Additionally, we can empower local communities to form patrol groups to monitor elephant movements and manage conflicts before those escalate.\n\nIn order to truly help our farmers and communities, the Government must streamline and improve compensation schemes. Payments should be timely and adequate, reflecting the true extent of the losses faced by affected families. Beyond compensation, we should also support alternative livelihoods, providing training and resources to help our communities diversify their income sources, whether through ecotourism or small-scale businesses.\n\nEducation and awareness are equally important. Conservation education in schools, mosques and community organizations can help build a deeper understanding of the importance of elephants in our ecosystem. At the same time, we can train farmers on peaceful coexistence strategies, arming them with the knowledge to protect their crops without resorting to violence.\n\nFinally, stronger policy and enforcement measures are necessary. Anti-poaching laws must be strictly enforced\n\nand new policies that address land use planning and elephant conservation must be developed with community input. Collaborative efforts between local communities, conservationists and policymakers would ensure that solutions are effective and sustainable."
  },
