    "chunk_size_bytes": 1556,
    "chunk_text": "Regarding the forestland issue, the position of the previous Government was to go back to the 1983 survey plan of the lands available in Sri Lanka. If we go back to that plan, some of these forestland issues can be resolved. So, we want to know the Government\u2019s position on that matter. The Minister of Justice and National Integration is here and we want to know about that because the demarcation of forestland is a big problem that we face in the North and the East. I am sure that the forestland issue is there in other parts of the country as well, but it is a major problem that we face in the North and the East.\n\n\u0b85\u0baa\u0ba4\u0baa\u0baa\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0bc8\u0bb1\u0bcd\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0ba4\u0bc8\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb0\u0bc1 \u0baa\u0bbf\u0bb0\u0b9a\u0bcd\u0b9a\u0bbf\u0bae\u0ba9 withdrawal of army camps. I would like to welcome the Government's action in that regard even though that extent of land was small. So, an army camp had been removed because it was not necessary and some road blocks had been removed. We welcome that. Thank you. Those are things that the previous Governments should have done. Also, some of the temples that were not accessible to the Tamil people, now, we can access. So, we thank you; we welcome the good initiatives of the Government. But, we want you to take further action on these matters. There are many army camps that need to be removed because they occupy State lands, schools and temples. So, we want the Government to take further action. While we welcome what you have already achieved, we want to encourage the Government to take further progressive steps in the right direction."
  },
