    "md5": "c7fc6fbd6502dc3a2c91af063c2acad5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1418,
    "chunk_text": "Debt restructuring has been in three stages. The first stage was Domestic Debt Restructuring, which happened on the 01st of July, 2023, where the domestic debt was looked at and a solution was provided. There were weak points which could be looked at and debated, but finally, that particular first step was taken with the least impact. Then, the second was, bilateral and multilateral debt. The Paris Club and the countries which are not in the Paris Club, all got together with the Official Creditor Committee co-chaired by Japan and India under the\n\nBy any chance, if this Agreement had not been fulfilled, then what would have been the implications to the country? We would have had to face the impact of economic instability, loss of investor confidence, social unrest, a humanitarian crisis and strained international relations. Then, on the other side, by restructuring debt, the benefits accrued to the country are, restored access to international markets -Sri Lanka had been shied away at that particular moment by creditors, where we were not able to even open LCs, Hon. Presiding Member- economic stability, IMF support, improved credit ratings and economic growth. Lack of those were the factors which basically led the country to totter at that particular moment with the leadership of the Government gone, and the responsibility fell on the then President Ranil Wickremasinghe to carry the baby forward."
